   "id": "03-valuation",
   "num": "03",
   "zh": "估值与安全边际",
   "en": "Valuation and Margin of Safety",
   "zh_sum": "四把尺子各自的适用边界，DCF 为什么只能给区间，安全边际该留多厚。",
   "en_sum": "Which multiple fits which business, why DCF only yields a range, and how deep the discount must be.",
   "books": [
    "congming-de-touzizhe",
    "zhengquan-fenxi",
    "zenyang-xuanze-chengzhanggu",
    "touzi-zhong-zui-zhongyao-de-shi"
   ],
   "zh_html": "<h2>一句话</h2>\n<p>估值不是算出精确数字，是判断\"这个价格买入，最坏情况我亏多少\"。安全边际是折扣，不是小数点。</p>\n<h2>四把尺子，各有适用场景</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>市盈率 PE</strong>：适合盈利稳定的公司。周期股在利润高点时 PE 最低，最危险。</li>\n<li><strong>市净率 PB</strong>：适合银行、保险这类资产说话的行业。轻资产公司用它没有意义。</li>\n<li><strong>EV/EBITDA</strong>：适合重资产、高负债，把资本结构差异拉平再比。</li>\n<li><strong>市销率 PS</strong>：只在亏损但高增长的阶段用，且必须配合毛利率判断。</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>现金流折现 DCF</strong> 理论上最严谨，实际上对假设极度敏感。折现率动一个点，结果能差三成。它的正确用法是给出一个区间，以及做敏感性分析看哪个假设最关键，而不是输出一个\"内在价值 87.3 元\"。</p>\n<h2>安全边际怎么做</h2>\n<p>先估一个保守的价值区间，只在市场价格明显低于区间下沿时买入。这个折价不是为了多赚，是为了给判断错误留出空间。</p>\n<p>折价要多少，取决于生意有多确定。稳定收租的资产折价两成足够，技术路线还没定的行业折价五成也未必够。</p>\n<h2>能力圈不是借口</h2>\n<p>说\"我看不懂\"很诚实，但不能对所有行业都说。能力圈是一个需要主动扩建的东西，路径通常是：先做一个行业，读五年财报，跟踪三家公司的季度数据，形成自己的判断框架，再横向复制。</p>\n<p>真正的边界不是行业，是可预测性。十年后这家公司还在卖同样的东西吗？如果答案模糊，就别用 DCF，改用更保守的资产法或直接放弃。</p>\n<h2>三个估值幻觉</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>便宜不等于好</strong>。低 PE 可能是在反映生意正在恶化，市场提前定价了。</li>\n<li><strong>高增长可以合理化任何价格</strong>。用 PEG 把四十倍 PE 说成合理，是牛市里最常见的自我说服。</li>\n<li><strong>历史估值区间可靠</strong>。行业结构变了，历史分位就没有参考价值。</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>自检</h2>\n<p>你买入的理由，能否用三句话说清，并且其中不包含\"别人都在买\"和\"应该会涨\"？</p>",
   "en_html": "<h2>In one sentence</h2>\n<p>Valuation is not a precise number. It is an answer to \"if I buy here, how much do I lose in the bad case\". Margin of safety is a discount, not a decimal place.</p>\n<h2>Four rulers, four jobs</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>P/E</strong> suits stable earnings. For cyclicals the lowest P/E often marks the peak of the cycle, which is exactly when it looks cheapest.</li>\n<li><strong>P/B</strong> suits banks and insurers, where assets carry the business. It says nothing about asset-light companies.</li>\n<li><strong>EV/EBITDA</strong> suits capital-intensive, leveraged businesses. It neutralises capital structure before comparing.</li>\n<li><strong>P/S</strong> only fits loss-making growth, and only alongside gross margin.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>DCF</strong> is the most rigorous in theory and the most sensitive in practice. Move the discount rate one point and the output moves thirty percent. Use it to produce a range and a sensitivity table that shows which assumption dominates, not a single \"intrinsic value of 87.3\".</p>\n<h2>How to set the discount</h2>\n<p>Estimate a conservative range first, then buy only when the market is clearly below the low end. The discount is not for extra return, it is for being wrong.</p>\n<p>How deep the discount must be depends on certainty. For a stable rent-collecting asset, twenty percent is plenty. For an industry whose technology is unsettled, fifty percent may not be enough.</p>\n<h2>Circle of competence is not an excuse</h2>\n<p>Saying \"I do not understand this\" is honest. Saying it about every industry is avoidance. A circle of competence is built, not discovered. The usual path: pick one industry, read five years of statements, track three companies quarterly, build your own framework, then copy the method sideways.</p>\n<p>The real boundary is not the sector, it is predictability. Will this company still sell the same thing in ten years? If that is fuzzy, skip DCF and use a conservative asset-based approach, or skip the company.</p>\n<h2>Three valuation illusions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cheap is not good.</strong> A low multiple can be the market pricing in deterioration ahead of the reports.</li>\n<li><strong>Growth justifies any price.</strong> Using PEG to bless forty times earnings is the most common self-persuasion in a bull market.</li>\n<li><strong>Historical ranges are reliable.</strong> Once industry structure changes, the percentile table is decoration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Self-check</h2>\n<p>Can you state your reason for buying in three sentences, without the words \"everyone is buying\" or \"it should go up\"?</p>",
